Azure Resource Manager (ARM) is the deployment and management layer that provides a consistent way to create, manage, organize, and control Azure resources. ARM acts as the management plane through which Azure resources can be deployed and governed.

Key capabilities include:

* Creating and managing Azure resources
* Organizing resources into resource groups
* Applying RBAC permissions
* Applying Azure Policy
* Adding resource tags
* Applying resource locks
* Managing deployments
* Supporting Infrastructure as Code

For example, when an administrator creates a virtual machine through the Azure portal, ARM coordinates the deployment of the VM and its associated resources such as disks, network interfaces, and networking components.

A real-life use case is an enterprise managing thousands of Azure resources through consistent ARM-based deployment and governance mechanisms.

Understanding ARM is important because many Azure management operations ultimately rely on Azure Resource Manager.
